# `cron run` is the one verb this sidecar schedules -- it decides
# internally which jobs (pdns sync, domain sync, domain reap-deletions,
# poll process, session keepalive) are actually due this tick, from each
# job's own `enabled`/`frequency_minutes` setting, and runs only those. See
# docs/INSTALL.md's "Scheduled jobs" for what each one does and how to turn
# it on (and, for `poll process`, off -- it's the only thing that acts on
# the registry's passwdReminder, on by default, but the operator's call).
#
# EPPITNIC_CONFIG_DIR/EPPITNIC_VAR_DIR are set as image ENV (see the
# Dockerfile) and would normally just be inherited, but cron implementations
# differ in what environment they hand a job -- vixie-style cron resets it to
# a fixed minimal set, and only variables declared in the crontab itself
# survive that. BusyBox crond's actual behaviour here isn't verified (no
# Docker on this machine to check), so they're declared explicitly below
# rather than assumed inherited. Do not "tidy up" this duplication with the
# Dockerfile's ENV -- without it, `cron run` looks for /app/config/config.php,
# finds nothing, and fails every minute: a scheduler that's silently broken
# on an otherwise healthy stack.
EPPITNIC_CONFIG_DIR=/data/config
EPPITNIC_VAR_DIR=/data/var

# su-exec, not root: config.php and the selftest notes this writes have to
# be owned the same as the web tier that also reads and writes them (see
# docker/entrypoint.sh's chown). /proc/1/fd/1 is this container's own PID 1
# (crond itself, run in the foreground -- see compose.yaml) -- the usual way
# to make a cron job's output land in `docker logs` instead of nowhere.
#
# Shipped unconditionally, once a minute: every job it might run is itself
# a no-op (prints nothing, exits 0) while its own `enabled` flag is off, so
# this needs no image change when an installation turns one on.
* * * * * su-exec www-data /usr/local/bin/eppitnic cron run >> /proc/1/fd/1 2>&1
